<http://purl.org/nanopub/temp/np1/assertion> {
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/hygromorph-antisymmetric-twist> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim> .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/hygromorph-antisymmetric-twist> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Antisymmetric fibre angles turn differential swelling into twist" .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/hygromorph-antisymmetric-twist> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/description> "When the two layers of a swelling bilayer have their stiff fibres at equal and opposite angles to the strip axis, the in-plane shear strains of the layers are equal and opposite, so the through-thickness mismatch is a pure shear mismatch that drives helical twisting rather than bending; this is how chiral seed pods and wheat awns open." .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/hygromorph-antisymmetric-twist> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/TestableHypothesis> .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/hygromorph-antisymmetric-twist> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation> "\\varepsilon_{12}^{(j)} = \\tfrac{1}{2}(\\varepsilon_L - \\varepsilon_T)\\sin 2\\phi_j; \\text{ with } \\phi_1 = +\\phi,\\ \\phi_2 = -\\phi: \\ \\Delta\\varepsilon_{12} = (\\varepsilon_L - \\varepsilon_T)\\sin 2\\phi, \\ \\Delta\\varepsilon_{11} = 0" .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/hygromorph-antisymmetric-twist> <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Assumptions: each layer swells transversely isotropically about its fibre direction with principal strains eps_L (along fibres) and eps_T (across); the strain tensor transforms as a rank-2 tensor under rotation by the fibre angle; the two layers have the same material and |angle|. Formal status: proved; the strain transformation is elementary tensor algebra and the resulting shear mismatch drives twist in the thin-plate limit (Armon et al. 2011, who also show the transition from helicoid to cylindrical helix as width grows; Marom et al. 2026, eq. 28). Which shape the strip adopts at finite width depends on nonlinear plate theory and is only partly closed-form." .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/hygromorph-antisymmetric-twist> <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/proposedTest> "In Bauhinia pods or wheat awns, measure the fibre angle of each layer and the swelling strains eps_L and eps_T of the tissue; predict the twist per unit length from the shear mismatch and the plate stiffness and compare with the pitch of the opened pod or awn." .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/hygromorph-antisymmetric-twist> <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/hypothesisType> "model-theorem" .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/hygromorph-antisymmetric-twist> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/source> "Shahaf Armon, Efi Efrati, Raz Kupferman & Eran Sharon (2011) Geometry and Mechanics in the Opening of Chiral Seed Pods" .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/hygromorph-antisymmetric-twist> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#seeAlso> <https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1140097> .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/hygromorph-antisymmetric-twist> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#seeAlso> <https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jmps.2026.106780> .
}
